      "problem": "Factor the expression $x^3 + x^2 - 2x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x & =x({x}^{2}+x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $x^2 + x - 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}+x-2) & = \\\\ x(({x}^{2}-x)+(2x-2)) & = \\\\ x(x(x-1)+2(x-1)) & = \\\\ x(x+2)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
